    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This essay won or received an honorable mention for The Boothe Prize for excellence in first-year writing. The Boothe Prize recognizes and rewards outstanding expository and argumentative writing by undergraduate students in first-year classes that satisfy the WR 1 requirement. In each award-winning essay, student writers demonstrate clarity of argument, excellent integration of research-based evidence, and compelling prose style.\r\nThis essay interrogates the pedagogical philosophy of the Venezuelan music education program El Sistema. The author looks beyond hagiographic accounts of El Sistema alums such as Gustavo Dudamel, bringing educator Paolo Friere’s theory in his book Pedagogy of the Oppressed to bear on his question of whether the El Sistema model can be applicable in the United States.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Hernandez, D. (2024). Humanization and Dialogue in El Sistema and American Adaptations: Socially Minded Music Education through a Freirian Lens. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/pt649py2302. https://doi.org/10.25740/pt649py2302.",
